        pages: [
          {
            title: 'Mentiroso (Liar)',
            subtitle: 'Card game',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: "Mentiroso (Spanish for 'Liar') is the Hispanic world's definitive version of the global bluffing game 'Cheat' or 'I Doubt It'. Played with the 40-card Spanish deck, it is a game of shedding and deception. Players discard cards face-down and claim their rank; if an opponent believes the player is lying, they shout 'Â¡Mentiroso!'. It is a cultural staple in Spain and Latin America, often played in families to teach social observation and poker-face maintenance.",
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Dates back centuries as a gambling folk game. The Spanish version is distinct due to the use of the 40-card deck (Oros, Copas, Espadas, Bastos).',
              },
            ],
          },
          {
            title: 'Setup',
            subtitle: 'Table, deck, equipment, and deal',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Two to six players; the game is often best with four. The entire 40-card Spanish deck is dealt out evenly so that each player has the same number of cards (or at most one card difference). Players should keep their card count visible.',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: '40-card Spanish deck (A, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, Sota, Caballo, Rey).',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: '40-card Spanish deck (A, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, Sota, Caballo, Rey).',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'The entire deck is dealt out evenly.',
              },
            ],
          },
